# HELP lith_open_handles Open file descriptors on the mount, counted per DESCRIPTOR across all processes including descriptors never read. This USED to be the readahead divisor, which is why holding files open shrank prefetch depth (#298); it no longer is (#301). Compare with lith_streaming_handles.
# TYPE lith_open_handles gauge
lith_open_handles 0
# HELP lith_prefetch_budget_bytes The --prefetch-budget limit in bytes (default 50%% of --mem-cache). lith_prefetch_committed_bytes is what is actually held against it.
# TYPE lith_prefetch_budget_bytes gauge
lith_prefetch_budget_bytes 4.127829504e+09
# HELP lith_prefetch_committed_bytes Bytes prefetch has committed and nothing has consumed, which is exactly resident-unread PLUS queued-for-a-slot PLUS on-the-wire. Charged at DISPATCH, before the prefetch and S3 semaphores, so --inflight-bytes does NOT bound it: that bounds the wire, not the queue. This is not a resident-memory figure. At rest it equals lith_prefetch_unread_resident_bytes exactly; use that one, not this, to judge memory-tier pressure (#313, #320).
# TYPE lith_prefetch_committed_bytes gauge
lith_prefetch_committed_bytes 0
# HELP lith_prefetch_coverage_held_total Reads that made CONTIGUOUS progress and were denied a readahead window anyway, because coverage had not confirmed the access tiles. This rising while lith_prefetch_low_coverage_total stays flat is the signature of #316: concurrent readers of ONE object have their siblings' reads absorbed by the shared kernel page cache, so each handle advances in order yet looks punctate and never establishes -- measured at 243x slower with byte amplification of 1.001. Nothing counted this before: deEstablish() only counts an establishment that existed, and these handles are refused while still provisional.
# TYPE lith_prefetch_coverage_held_total counter
lith_prefetch_coverage_held_total 1
# HELP lith_prefetch_deestablished_total Times a handle lost an establishment it had, by object size class (#256). NOT the same as lith_prefetch_reset_random_total, which counts collapses to the Random state and is 20-30x larger; a high value here tracks a reader whose prefetch works (frequent productive re-anchoring), not a wasteful one.
# TYPE lith_prefetch_deestablished_total counter
lith_prefetch_deestablished_total{size_class=">64MiB"} 0
# HELP lith_prefetch_evicted_unread_total Prefetched chunks evicted before a demand read consumed them (thrash; #55).
# TYPE lith_prefetch_evicted_unread_total counter
lith_prefetch_evicted_unread_total 0
# HELP lith_prefetch_evidence_clamped_total Times the #256 evidence gate held a readahead window below the configured max, by object size class. Zero unless --readahead-evidence-ratio is set.
# TYPE lith_prefetch_evidence_clamped_total counter
lith_prefetch_evidence_clamped_total{size_class=">64MiB"} 57
# HELP lith_prefetch_evidence_withheld_blocks_total Readahead blocks the #256 evidence gate withheld (window the detector wanted minus the window consumption earned).
# TYPE lith_prefetch_evidence_withheld_blocks_total counter
lith_prefetch_evidence_withheld_blocks_total 6439
# HELP lith_prefetch_issued_total Chunks prefetched (1 MiB cache chunks, not blocks -- one record per chunk dispatched).
# TYPE lith_prefetch_issued_total counter
lith_prefetch_issued_total 1149
# HELP lith_prefetch_low_coverage_total Reads the #221 coverage gate forced Random on a SEEK landing: a scattered walk, which is the gate working as designed. Incremented live, per read. Pair with lith_prefetch_coverage_held_total -- that one rising while this stays flat is the #316 shape (#221, #316).
# TYPE lith_prefetch_low_coverage_total counter
lith_prefetch_low_coverage_total 0
# HELP lith_prefetch_pressure Outstanding prefetch commitment as a fraction of the MEMORY TIER's realized capacity -- the quantity --prefetch-pressure-max admits against (#313). Unbounded above by design: 8.0 means eight tiers' worth has been promised and most of it must evict something unread on arrival. Read it with lith_prefetch_unread_resident_bytes, which saturates at the tier and so cannot tell a mild overcommit from a 12x one, and with lith_prefetch_pressure_held_total, which says whether the gate is actually doing anything.
# TYPE lith_prefetch_pressure gauge
lith_prefetch_pressure 0
# HELP lith_prefetch_pressure_held_total Prefetch dispatches DROPPED by the pressure gate (#313). Zero means either the gate is off or it never had to fire, and those are different states -- read lith_prefetch_pressure to tell them apart. Dropping a dispatch costs nothing a reader waits on: demand reads do not go through the prefetch path, so under pressure lith stops guessing and keeps serving.
# TYPE lith_prefetch_pressure_held_total counter
lith_prefetch_pressure_held_total 0
# HELP lith_prefetch_reset_random_total Prefetch detector collapses to random (two seeks, no progress between).
# TYPE lith_prefetch_reset_random_total counter
lith_prefetch_reset_random_total 0
